```
\documentclass{article}
% Load toc package and set up TOC environment
\usepackage[title]{toctree}

\tocset{
  tocdic={%
    \listoftodos,
    % Add more options here if needed, e.g., listof files or sections.
  }
}

% Define main content section with headings
\begin{document}
\tableofcontents

% Generate sub-sections from main content
\section*{Subsections}
\subsubsection{Section one}
\subsubsection{Section two}
\end{document}
```

In this example, we first load the `toc` package by adding `\usepackage[toc]{toctree}` at the beginning of our document class file (`article`). We then define a new command called `tocs`, which sets up the basic structure of the TOC.

Next, we use the `tocset` option to add custom options such as lists of todos that will appear on each page of the TOC. You may also want to customize other aspects of the TOC, such as including links to specific pages within the document or formatting it differently depending on whether you're viewing the TOC directly or through another means like BibTeX.

Finally, we create a main body section where all of the actual text goes, along with any additional information about what's included in the TOC. This includes heading levels, titles, and optional notes or references.

